# Veripsa release gate (auto-discovered). One gate per file -> two PRs adding gates add two FILES, never a
# shared line -> no false merge conflict on registration. register_gate is defined in run_gates.sh.
# Format: register_gate <test> <marker> <name> <ok_blurb> <desc> [tail]   (no dollar/backtick in the text)
register_gate "tests/test_cochange_denominator.py" "CO-CHANGE DENOMINATOR GATE: PASS" "co-change honest denominator" "graph_insights_for_installation.coupled now carries the HONEST DENOMINATOR pair support_n + observed_m alongside pct, so the platform dashboard can render 'N of M observed commits . X%' instead of a bare % that LIES on a thin sample (a single 1-of-1 co-occurrence has strength=1.0 -> pct=100, indistinguishable from a 40-of-55 coupling). support_n = co (commits where BOTH files changed); observed_m = n_a+n_b-co (the UNION = commits where EITHER changed = the commits in which the pair was OBSERVABLE, by inclusion-exclusion |A union B| = |A|+|B|-|A intersect B| with the intersection = co). M is the pair-local union, NOT n_total (the whole-repo count), so a quiet pair is not diluted by the repo's overall volume. The gate stands up the ephemeral Postgres + a fixture (ACCT-DEMO inst 111, one repo, 4 floor-passing co-change pairs hand-built so a LOW and a HIGH pair share the SAME bare pct=75 but have support_n/observed_m of 3/5 vs 30/50) and proves live: (D1) every coupled pair carries integer support_n AND observed_m; (D2) support_n==co and observed_m==n_a+n_b-co exactly for hand-checked rows, and no pair's observed_m equals the whole-repo n_total (the denominator is the union, never the global count); (D3) observed_m>=support_n>=1 for every pair (a union can never be below its intersection, so the honest 'N of M' can never read above 100%); (D4) the LOW and HIGH pairs render the identical pct yet are told apart by the denominator (the thin coupling is now self-evidently thin -- the lie the old {a,b,pct}-only surface told); (D5) a genuine 1-of-1 (co=1, strength=1.0 -- the classic bare-100% masquerade) and a co=2 pair stay BELOW the co>=3 floor and never surface (the floor is the first defense, the denominator the second). Content-free: support_n/observed_m are BEHAVIORAL co-occurrence COUNTS for the customer's OWN repo in the AUTHENTICATED dashboard (the product's visible effect), NOT the graph mechanism -- a distinct channel from the GitHub PR-check string where the raw '(N of M changes)' parenthetical is banned (gate 184)." "Adds support_n + observed_m to core.graph_insights_for_installation.coupled (additive -- the existing {a,b,pct} keys are unchanged; only two integer COUNT fields are appended) so the hosted dashboard shows an honest 'N of M observed commits . X%' coupled line. support_n=co (BOTH changed), observed_m=n_a+n_b-co (the UNION = EITHER changed = the observation basis); the union (not n_total) is the statistically-correct denominator -- 'of the commits touching either file, how many touched both' -- and it makes a thin coupling self-evident (a 1-of-1 would read '1 of 1', exposing the sample size the bare pct hides). The change is in the SQL surface only (the inner co_change subquery already had co; n_a/n_b are pulled alongside) -- render.py / webhook.py / the PR-check path are untouched, so the gate-184 PR-check raw-count ban is unaffected. The gate proves the RPC returns support_n+observed_m per coupled pair AND that a low-support pair is distinguishable from a high-support one (same pct, different N/M), on the live ephemeral Postgres. Content-free (paths + counts only)." 30
